mi spuda la lojbab. di'e

The logico-semantic nuances of negation are notoriously difficult to
understand.

Our discussion has produced several sample utterances in which other-
than-selbri fails to entail not-bridi.  In fact, we've shown that
selbri and other-than-selbri can both be true for the same sumti
simultaneously.  The question that remains is this:  Do these sample
utterances use the word {na'e} correctly?  More succinctly:  Does
{na'e} really mean "other than"?

